What:		/sys/class/drm/card*/device/agx_stats
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	Asahi Linux GPU driver maintainers
Description:
		Read-only dump of the AGX firmware statistics decoded by the
		drm/asahi driver. The driver already polls the firmware statistics
		ring (see `drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/channel.rs::StatsChannel::poll`,
		`drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/fw/channels.rs::StatsMsg`) and exposes the
		latest values via this file.

		The file is owned by the platform device
		`/sys/devices/platform/<...>.gpu/`, which is symlinked from the
		`device` entry of the matching DRM card under `/sys/class/drm/`.

		One `key value` pair per line. Lines are ASCII integer values
		unless noted. Unknown keys may be appended in future firmware
		versions and must be ignored by readers.

		Module parameter `asahi.stats_export` (default 1) controls
		whether the driver retains the values. When 0, the file shows
		`unsupported` (the firmware messages are still polled but
		discarded).

		Access: Read-only. Owner-readable (mode 0444).

		busy_ns    cumulative u64; utilization-weighted busy time in
		           nanoseconds since boot: each Utilization message window
		           contributes its duration scaled by the busiest subqueue
		           percentage. Firmware timestamps are base-clock ticks
		           (HwConfig.base_clock_hz, 24 MHz on every supported SoC)
		           and are converted to ns by the driver. Monotonic;
		           bounded by elapsed wall time. MEASURED on T6021
		           (M2 Max, 13.5): idle desktop fraction ~0.01, saturated
		           4096^2 fp16 matmul fraction ~1.0 (util1..4 = 100).
		           T6001 shares the message format and the 24 MHz clock
		           (fw 13.5); the same idle-vs-matmul check must be run
		           there before the magnitude claim is repeated.

		jobs       u64; cumulative completed submissions since boot.
		           Counted on the host at submission completion (when
		           the last command of a submission completes and its
		           fence signals), not from a firmware counter.

		pstate     u32; current performance state index (PowerState.pstate).

		requested_frequency_hz
		           G13/G14: nominal OPP frequency in Hz for the pstate
		           printed in this read. Uses the driver's parsed
		           operating-points-v2 table in firmware order, without
		           sorting. This is not a measured achieved frequency.
		           Unsupported if the index has no table entry.
		           All pre-existing keys retain their format and order;
		           this key is appended after temperature_scale.

		power_mw   u32; latest average power reading in milliwatts
		           (AvgPower.avg_power). May be 0 if the firmware has not
		           yet reported AvgPower.

		util1..util4   u32; raw `Utilization` fields from the firmware,
		           unscaled. Semantics (firmware tick vs % vs MHz) are
		           validated against a controlled MLX load and may
		           differ per SoC revision. Treat as raw until a per-chip
		           doc note lands.

		temperature_raw, temperature_scale    u32; latest Temperature
		           sample and scale (or 0 if Temperature has not yet
		           arrived).

		Field meanings (units, window, saturation) are documented in
		`drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/stats.rs` and may evolve with firmware
		revisions. busy_ns is monotonic non-decreasing while the device
		is bound (it only accumulates). The snapshot struct is
		`#[repr(C)]` on the Rust side and its offsets are asserted at
		build time in `sysfs.c`; readers must parse `key value` lines and
		must not assume binary layout of this file.

		G16S (T6040/V25G83) and G17P (T8140/V26A428) use a separate,
		qualified snapshot. The existing G13/G14 keys above are unchanged.
		The following keys replace the legacy keys on these firmwares:

		utilisation_status, pstate_status
		           One of valid, stale, unavailable or unsupported.
		           unavailable means no qualified sample in the current
		           epoch yet (for example right after boot, resume or
		           a firmware restart); stale means the last sample is
		           older than the age limit below or its epoch ended.
		           unsupported is a fixed property of the firmware,
		           taken from the driver's per-firmware match data, and
		           never changes at runtime: the V25G83 and V26A428
		           firmwares accept tag 0x10 but never emit it, and no
		           other record carries the current pstate, so
		           pstate_status always reads unsupported on G16S and
		           G17P. utilisation_status is never unsupported on
		           these firmwares. Numeric metrics are printed only
		           while their capability is valid; otherwise their
		           value is the string unsupported.

		util_tiler_percent, util_renderer_percent, util_device_percent
		           Unsigned integer percent (0..100), from tag 0x13
		           words 3, 4 and 6 respectively (zero-based). Device
		           utilisation is independent of the overlapping engines;
		           it is never their sum. Each complete valid packet from
		           either role contributes one sample to a shared integer
		           mean, rounded down. Reads do not consume samples.
		           A packet with any value above 100 invalidates the whole
		           utilisation capability. Zero is a valid sample.

		pstate     Logical selector 0..10 from tag 0x10 word 6, on a
		           firmware whose match data says it emits tag 0x10
		           (none of the current G16S/G17P firmwares do). Words 1
		           and 2 form a little-endian u64 firmware timestamp.
		           The first timestamp establishes a baseline. Invalid
		           selectors and backwards timestamps remove pstate
		           validity; timestamps are never extended across epochs.
		           P0 is a logical state, not a core-power measurement.

		requested_frequency_hz, achieved_frequency_hz, busy_ns,
		power_mw, temperature_raw
		           Always unsupported on these firmwares. The statistics
		           packet does not identify performance table A or B;
		           a selector is neither a host request nor a measured
		           frequency. No qualified busy-time duration, power unit
		           or temperature field is available.

		M5 firmware (the M5 command set of the shared G17 backend) uses
		the same snapshot and keys, with these differences:

		pstate_status, pstate
		           Supported. The firmware reports the performance state
		           in effect in tag 0x1b, words 1 and 3, which must
		           agree: an index 1..13 into the board's performance
		           state table. The end of the interval record before it
		           (tag 0x19, words 1 and 2) is its firmware timestamp.
		           A report that names state 0 or a state beyond the
		           table removes pstate validity. The state reads stale
		           while the GPU cores are gated.

		achieved_frequency_hz
		           The frequency of pstate in the board's performance
		           state table, in Hz, while pstate_status is valid.

		busy_ns    Cumulative u64 since the driver was bound. The tag
		           0x13 records of this firmware carry a timestamp in
		           24 MHz ticks (words 1 and 2). Each record credits the
		           window since the previous record of its epoch at its
		           device utilisation. A record more than two 8 ms
		           periods after the previous one, or with an earlier
		           timestamp, only starts a new window, so time the GPU
		           spent gated is never credited. MEASURED on T6050
		           (J716c): 871 and 863 ms for three 300 ms compute jobs
		           at full clock (905 and 919 ms of work).

		requested_frequency_hz, power_mw, temperature_raw
		           Unsupported.

		The host uses a 16 ms age/averaging-window limit for V25G83 and
		32 ms for V26A428 (two nominal periods). These are conservative
		host policies, not firmware guarantees about packet duration.
		The oldest contributing utilisation sample bounds its window.
		The firmware can publish several records per producer update
		(V25G83 produces one every 8 ms and the host receives them three
		at a time, every 24 ms), so the age limit is added to the
		shorter of the last two delivery intervals seen in the current
		epoch: utilisation reads stale only once the next delivery is
		late by the limit, never between two on-time deliveries.
		A logical pstate has no qualified periodic-report cadence and
		remains valid until a replacement or an epoch boundary; its
		host receive time is retained in diagnostics, not used to invent
		a periodic-report deadline.
		Suspend/resume, restart, statistics-ring quarantine, observed
		core gating and teardown end validity. After an epoch boundary,
		each ring's already queued records are discarded before new
		baselines are accepted. A quarantined statistics ring disables
		both roles' exports until reprobe; a trace-ring error does not.

		Reads take the device mutex and do not wake the GPU. The
		firmware_reports debugfs view retains per-role raw packets,
		counts and host monotonic receive times, plus capability status,
		policy, timestamp and diagnostic tick deltas. Tick deltas are
		never accumulated as GPU busy time. Firmware role streams are
		merged in drain order without deduplication.
		The diagnostic also identifies the platform device, records the
		transport-admitted count and last-admission decision per role/tag,
		and shows suspension, queued-record discard and quarantine state.
		Transport admission does not imply semantic validity: the decoder
		still checks each percentage, selector and timestamp.
